# Topos self-host configuration — the whole surface, in one file.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `docker compose` reads `.env` from beside `docker-compose.yml` automatically: no flag, no export.
# The two BLANK assignments below are the only edits a laptop trial needs. Everything under them is
# commented out and shows the value already in force, so you never have to read the docs to find out
# what is settable — this file is the list.

# ── the two secrets — required, no defaults ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are deliberately no shipped values for these: a default secret in a public repository is a
# secret everyone has. Leave either one blank and `docker compose` REFUSES TO PARSE the file — the
# error names the variable, in your terminal, before a single container starts. (The app carries the
# same refusal at boot for deployments that do not come through compose.)

# Session signing (Better Auth). At least 32 characters; paste one `openssl rand -hex 32` output:
TOPOS_WEB_AUTH_SECRET=

# The internal-lane bearer — the ONE credential the vault and the gateway accept from the app, on the
# compose network only. Paste another `openssl rand -hex 32` output (a different one):
TOPOS_INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# ── the public origin — required for a real deployment ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one public base every client-visible URL rides: the resource addresses, the protocol card's
# `api_base_url` a CLI re-roots onto, the sign-in and invite links. Behind a TLS-terminating reverse
# proxy the app CANNOT infer it (the proxy speaks plain HTTP to the container), so it would otherwise
# publish an `http://` base the CLI refuses to downgrade onto. Left unset, the app answers on the
# request's own origin — which is what keeps a laptop `up` reachable from another machine on the LAN.
# TOPOS_PUBLIC_URL=https://topos.example.com

# The first-boot workspace's address slug — lowercase letters, digits and hyphens. It only names the
# workspace the install is born with; renaming later is a setting in the product.
# TOPOS_WORKSPACE_NAME=team

# ── the MCP gateway's public origin — required to put agents through the gateway ───────────────────
# The gateway runs either way; this is what TURNS IT ON for your team. Set, the app hands every machine
# one gateway address per connected MCP server: a person signs into that server ONCE on the web, and
# every agent in the workspace gets working tools with no per-machine sign-in. Unset, MCP servers are
# delivered in the direct shape and each machine signs in for itself — and clearing this variable again
# is the whole rollback.
#
# It is the gateway's OWN public origin, not the app's: a second hostname pointed at the same box (or
# the same hostname on port 8788, which the compose file publishes). It is also the base of the OAuth
# redirect URI upstream services send a person's browser back to, so it must be the address a browser
# can actually reach — and https for any real deployment.
# TOPOS_GATEWAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://gateway.topos.example.com

# Let the gateway dial MCP servers on your own network. Off by default: it refuses loopback, private
# and link-local addresses, which is what stops a pasted URL from turning the gateway into a probe of
# the network it runs in. Set to 1 only if the servers your team connects are genuinely internal.
# TOPOS_GATEWAY_ALLOW_PRIVATE_UPSTREAMS=0

# How many days of usage rows the gateway keeps — one row per call an agent proxied through it, which
# is what the usage page renders. 0, the value in force, keeps every row FOREVER: that table is the
# record of what your agents did, and an upgrade never deletes it behind you. Set a number of days and
# an hourly sweep removes rows past that age in small batches. 90 is the one to reach for first — a
# quarter outlives the longest range the usage page offers and any question a per-call row answers,
# and it keeps the table proportional to a season's traffic rather than to this deployment's whole life.
# TOPOS_GATEWAY_USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# ── outbound mail — optional, all five or none ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bring your own SMTP. Armed, the app sends invitations, address verification and password-reset
# mail. Unarmed, mail is simply off and the core loop is undamaged — sign-in, publishing and the
# claim ceremony all work — but a SECOND person cannot join: an invited teammate proves their address
# through the mailbox. Port 465 is implicit TLS; anything else (587) is STARTTLS. The user, pass and
# from are credentials-adjacent — they never appear in a log or an error.
# TOPOS_MAIL_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# TOPOS_MAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
# TOPOS_MAIL_SMTP_USER=topos@example.com
# TOPOS_MAIL_SMTP_PASS=
# TOPOS_MAIL_SMTP_FROM=Topos <topos@example.com>

# ── the release the three images are pinned to ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `docker-compose.yml` already pins all three images to the release this checkout was cut for, and the
# normal way to upgrade is to check out a newer tag — the compose file, the database's provisioning
# script and the pins move together. Set this only to run a different release than the tree you have
# (or `latest`, if you would rather track it and take the upgrade discipline on yourself).
# TOPOS_VERSION=v0.1.36

# ── the bundled database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The `db` service publishes NO port: it is reachable only from the application containers on the
# compose network, which is why these defaults are acceptable as they stand for the bundled Postgres.
# Set them on the FIRST `up` or not at all — the roles and the database are created once, on a fresh
# volume, and a later change would only lock the applications out. (A managed/external Postgres is a
# different recipe; see the `docker-compose.yml` header.)
#
# The three application roles: the plane owns schema `plane`, the web app owns schema `web`, the
# gateway owns schema `gateway`.
# TOPOS_PLANE_DB_PASSWORD=plane
# TOPOS_WEB_DB_PASSWORD=web
# TOPOS_GATEWAY_DB_PASSWORD=gateway
# The superuser the image's initdb creates, and the database all three applications live in. The
# superuser owns nothing any application uses; it exists to create the three roles above.
# POSTGRES_USER=topos
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=topos
# POSTGRES_DB=topos

# ── the vault's object store ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where every bundle byte lives. `local` (the default) is the plane-data volume — zero extra
# config, and an existing deployment's volume keeps working. `s3` keeps the bytes on any
# S3-compatible bucket (Cloudflare R2, MinIO, AWS S3) instead: the vault is then stateless over
# Postgres + the bucket. Switching an existing deployment runs the one-shot
# `docker compose run --rm -e TOPOS_PLANE_STORE=s3 plane import-local` first — see
# /docs/self-host, "The object store".
# TOPOS_PLANE_STORE=local
# TOPOS_PLANE_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# TOPOS_PLANE_S3_BUCKET=
# TOPOS_PLANE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# TOPOS_PLANE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# TOPOS_PLANE_S3_REGION=auto
# Upload staging is an EPHEMERAL local directory — no volume required; losing it on a container
# replacement loses only in-flight uploads, which clients replay. Created 0700 and refused if a
# symlink or a foreign-owned dir squats the path. Default: `.staging` under the local store root
# (the compose file pins /data/staging), or a per-uid temp dir on the s3 backend.
# TOPOS_PLANE_TMP=/data/staging

# ── presetting the claim ceremony — CI / IaC ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset, a fresh high-entropy claim code is minted on every boot while the workspace is unclaimed and
# printed to the app's logs as one `→ Finish setup: …` line; opening it seats the first owner. Preset
# it (at least 16 characters) when a machine, not a person, has to finish setup — only its SHA-256 is
# ever stored, and the first use consumes it. The link file mirrors that one printed line onto a path
# inside the web container, for operators without log access — it needs a volume mounted there.
# TOPOS_SETUP_CODE=
# TOPOS_SETUP_LINK_FILE=/data/setup-link.txt
